Multiple `.attr()` callbacks (for `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`) each independently call `positions.get(link.source)!`, `positions.get(link.target)!`, and `data.nodes.find(...)!`. Beyond being four separate O(n) `find` calls per link per attribute, the non-null `!` assertions on `Map.get()` results are disallowed by the project's coding rules. The `validLinks` filter above already confirms both endpoints exist in `positions`, so cache the results with a guard:
```ts
.each(function(link) {
  const sourcePos = positions.get(link.source);
  const targetPos = positions.get(link.target);
  if (!sourcePos || !targetPos) { return; }
  // set attributes via d3.select(this)
})
```

`const textElements: any[] = [];` uses an explicit `any` type. The elements being pushed are D3 `Selection` objects. Type them properly, e.g.:
```ts
import type { Selection } from 'd3';
const textElements: Selection<SVGTextElement, unknown, null, undefined>[] = [];
```
This prevents silent `.attr()` misuse and satisfies the project's no-`any` preference.

